WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:07.047
Press a button. Shake it. Clap your hands.

2
00:00:07.267 --> 00:00:11.168
Bitsy is a small computer you
can hold, and it feels all three.

3
00:00:11.668 --> 00:00:16.730
Bitsy is a physical computing device:
a small computer with real parts on it.

4
00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:21.269
Button A is an input. Press it,
and the program can feel it.

5
00:00:21.489 --> 00:00:25.343
The shake sensor feels a shake.
The microphone hears a clap.

6
00:00:25.563 --> 00:00:29.929
Twenty-five little lights, a speaker,
a motor and a bell are outputs.

7
00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:33.957
Bitsy has no screen, but it
runs programs. It is a computer.

8
00:00:34.177 --> 00:00:38.821
The micro:bit is a real board with
two buttons and a grid of lights.

9
00:00:39.321 --> 00:00:43.408
A device program starts with a
when block. It names the input.

10
00:00:43.628 --> 00:00:48.179
When button A is pressed. That is
the input this program waits for.

11
00:00:48.399 --> 00:00:51.231
Then come the outputs: show a heart, beep.

12
00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:54.331
Press A, and Bitsy
draws a heart and beeps.

13
00:00:54.551 --> 00:00:59.427
Shake it instead and nothing happens.
The program is waiting for A.

14
00:00:59.927 --> 00:01:03.271
Each output block makes
Bitsy do one real thing.

15
00:01:03.491 --> 00:01:06.974
All lights on turns every one
of the twenty-five lights on.

16
00:01:07.194 --> 00:01:11.095
Show a smile draws a smile.
All lights off makes them dark.

17
00:01:11.315 --> 00:01:15.169
Beep uses the speaker. Turn
the motor. Ring the bell.

18
00:01:15.389 --> 00:01:19.940
When the input arrives, the outputs
run in order, one after another.

19
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.898
The algorithm says show a heart.
You pressed A and got a smile.

20
00:01:25.118 --> 00:01:29.159
Do not give up. Change the
smile block for a heart block.

21
00:01:29.379 --> 00:01:33.140
Press A again to test it.
Now Bitsy shows a heart.

22
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.797
Testing a program and fixing
it is called debugging.

23
00:01:37.017 --> 00:01:40.500
If nothing happens at all, the
when block has the wrong input.

24
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.179
A program is not only for
screens. It moves real things too.

25
00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:50.043
Through a motor or a switch, traffic
lights change and a door opens.

26
00:01:50.263 --> 00:01:55.604
A washing machine turns its drum. A
lift climbs to the floor you pressed.

27
00:01:55.824 --> 00:01:59.725
Inside each one is a small
computer following a program.

28
00:02:00.225 --> 00:02:03.151
Every control system has
the same three parts.

29
00:02:03.371 --> 00:02:07.504
Something senses: a button,
a sensor, a microphone.

30
00:02:07.724 --> 00:02:11.253
Something decides: the
program says what to do.

31
00:02:11.473 --> 00:02:15.096
Something acts: a light,
a motor, a speaker.

32
00:02:15.316 --> 00:02:18.334
Try it on a shop door
that opens as you walk up.

33
00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:22.549
A press, a shake and a clap
are inputs Bitsy can feel.

34
00:02:22.769 --> 00:02:27.738
The when block goes first and names
the input. Then the outputs run.

35
00:02:27.958 --> 00:02:32.417
Test it, fix it, and a program
can control a real machine.
